.SH NAME
linscp \- graphical file-transfer client for SFTP, SCP, FTP, WebDAV and S3
.SH SYNOPSIS
.SY linscp
[\fIswitch\fR...] [\fIsession\fR]
.YS
.SY linscp
[\fIswitch\fR...] \fIsession\fR \fB/upload\fR \fIfile\fR...
.YS
.SY linscp
[\fIswitch\fR...] \fIsession\fR \fB/synchronize\fR [\fIlocal\-directory\fR [\fIremote\-directory\fR]] [\fB/defaults\fR]
.YS
.SY linscp
[\fIswitch\fR...] \fIsession\fR \fB/keepuptodate\fR [\fIlocal\-directory\fR [\fIremote\-directory\fR]] [\fB/defaults\fR]
.YS
.SY linscp
[\fIswitch\fR...] \fIsession\fR \fB/edit\fR \fIremote\-file\fR
.YS
.SY linscp
\fB/help\fR
.YS
.SH DESCRIPTION
.PP
\fBlinscp\fR is a two-panel file-transfer client that moves files between the local computer and remote servers over SFTP, SCP, FTP (plain or with TLS), WebDAV and Amazon S3.
It edits remote files, synchronizes directories, keeps a remote directory up to date with a local one, and stores sites in a Site Manager.
Its sites, settings and host keys are shared with \fBlinscp\-cli\fR(1), the console and scripting client.
.PP
Without arguments, \fBlinscp\fR opens the Site Manager, unless the preferences turn that off.
The optional \fIsession\fR names a site saved in the Site Manager, a folder or workspace of sites (each opens in a tab), or a session URL of the form \fIprotocol\fR\fB://\fR[\fIuser\fR[\fB:\fR\fIpassword\fR]\fB@\fR]\fIhost\fR[\fB:\fR\fIport\fR][\fB/\fR\fIpath\fR], where \fIprotocol\fR is one of \fBsftp\fR, \fBscp\fR, \fBftp\fR, \fBftps\fR, \fBftpes\fR, \fBdav\fR, \fBdavs\fR or \fBs3\fR.
A URL that names a file downloads it.
.PP
A session started while \fBlinscp\fR already runs opens in the running window, or in a new one, as the preferences say.
Hold Shift while starting to get the other behaviour.
.PP
Switches start with a slash or a hyphen:
\fB/upload\fR and \fB\-upload\fR are the same.
Their names are not case-sensitive.
A value follows an equals sign; switches that take a list take the arguments that follow, up to the next switch.
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
\fB/upload\fR \fIfile\fR...
Connect to \fIsession\fR and upload the files to its remote directory, showing the transfer dialog first.
.TP
\fB/uploadifany\fR \fIfile\fR...
As \fB/upload\fR, but start normally when no file is given.
.TP
\fB/synchronize\fR [\fIlocal\-directory\fR [\fIremote\-directory\fR]]
Connect to \fIsession\fR and open the Synchronize dialog for the two directories.
.TP
\fB/keepuptodate\fR [\fIlocal\-directory\fR [\fIremote\-directory\fR]]
Connect to \fIsession\fR and open the Keep Remote Directory up to Date dialog for the two directories.
.TP
\fB/defaults\fR
With \fB/upload\fR, \fB/synchronize\fR or \fB/keepuptodate\fR, start the operation with its default settings, without asking.
.TP
\fB/edit\fR \fIremote\-file\fR
Connect to \fIsession\fR and open \fIremote\-file\fR in the editor.
.TP
\fB/browse\fR
With a session URL that names a file, select that file in the remote panel instead of downloading it.
\fB/explore\fR is a synonym.
.TP
\fB/refresh\fR [\fIremote\-directory\fR]
Ask the running instance to reload the remote directory of \fIsession\fR, then exit.
.TP
\fB/newinstance\fR
Open \fIsession\fR in a new window even when \fBlinscp\fR already runs.
.TP
\fB/workspace\fR
When a site and a workspace share the name given as \fIsession\fR, open the workspace.
.TP
\fB/privatekey=\fR\fIfile\fR
SSH private key to authenticate with, for the session given and for sites that have none.
.TP
\fB/hostkey=\fR\fIfingerprint\fR
Fingerprint the server's SSH host key must have.
.TP
\fB/username=\fR\fIuser\fR
User name to log in with, used instead of one in the site or URL.
.TP
\fB/password=\fR\fIpassword\fR
Password to log in with.
Other users of the machine may see it in the process list.
.TP
\fB/passwordsfromfiles\fR
Treat the values of the password and passphrase switches as names of files that hold the secret.
.TP
\fB/clientcert=\fR\fIfile\fR
TLS client certificate file.
.TP
\fB/certificate=\fR\fIfingerprint\fR
Fingerprint the server's TLS certificate must have.
.TP
\fB/passphrase=\fR\fIpassphrase\fR
Passphrase that unlocks an encrypted private key or client certificate.
.TP
\fB/passive\fR[\fB=on\fR|\fBoff\fR]
FTP passive mode (the default) or active mode.
.TP
\fB/implicit\fR
Implicit TLS for FTP.
.TP
\fB/explicit\fR
Explicit TLS for FTP.
.TP
\fB/timeout=\fR\fIseconds\fR
How many seconds to wait for a server reply.
.TP
\fB/rawsettings\fR \fIname\fR\fB=\fR\fIvalue\fR...
Set any site setting by its raw name, as stored in the configuration file.
.TP
\fB/rawtransfersettings\fR \fIname\fR\fB=\fR\fIvalue\fR...
Set the default transfer settings of this run by their raw names.
.TP
\fB/rawconfig\fR \fIname\fR\fB=\fR\fIvalue\fR...
Set any application setting by its raw name.
.TP
\fB/ini=\fR\fIfile\fR
Read and save settings in this file instead of the default configuration file (see FILES).
With \fBnul\fR, start from defaults and save nothing.
.TP
\fB/log=\fR\fIfile\fR
Write a session log to this file.
.TP
\fB/loglevel=\fR\fIlevel\fR
How much detail the log holds, from \fB0\fR to \fB2\fR.
A trailing \fB*\fR also records passwords.
.TP
\fB/logsize=\fR[\fIcount\fR\fB*\fR]\fIsize\fR
Start a new log file when the current one reaches the size, keeping at most the given number of old files.
.TP
\fB/xmllog=\fR\fIfile\fR
Write an XML log of each operation to this file.
.TP
\fB/unsafe\fR
Mark the command line as coming from an untrusted source, such as a link opened in a web browser.
\fB/ini\fR, \fB/rawconfig\fR, \fB/xmllog\fR and \fB/defaults\fR are then ignored.
.TP
\fB\-\-lang=\fR\fIcode\fR
Use this interface language, such as \fBde\fR or \fBpt_BR\fR, instead of the one chosen in the preferences.
.TP
\fB/help\fR
Show a usage summary of \fBlinscp\-cli\fR(1) and exit.
\fB/info\fR, \fB/keygen\fR, \fB/copyid\fR, \fB/fingerprintscan\fR and \fB/batchsettings\fR also run \fBlinscp\-cli\fR(1) with the same command line.
.SH FILES
.TP
\fB~/.config/linscp/WinSCP.ini\fR
Sites, preferences and remembered host keys, shared with \fBlinscp\-cli\fR(1).
The directory is \fB$XDG_CONFIG_HOME/linscp\fR when that variable is set.
It is created when needed.
.TP
\fIprogram\-directory\fR\fB/WinSCP.ini\fR
When this file exists beside the program, it is used instead, which makes a portable copy.
For an AppImage, the file goes beside the \fB.AppImage\fR file.
.TP
\fB~/.var/app/org.linscp.linscp/config/linscp/WinSCP.ini\fR
The configuration file of the Flatpak, which shares nothing with other installs and never uses a file beside its program.
.SH ENVIRONMENT
.TP
.B XDG_CONFIG_HOME
The base directory of the configuration file; \fB~/.config\fR when unset.
.TP
.B HOME
The user's home directory.
.TP
.B TMPDIR
Temporary files go to a private per-user directory under it; \fB/tmp\fR when unset.
.TP
.B APPIMAGE
Set by an AppImage to its own path.
A \fBWinSCP.ini\fR beside that file makes the configuration portable.
.TP
.B SSH_AUTH_SOCK
The SSH agent used for public key authentication.
.TP
.B WINSCP_PATH
Set by linscp, for scripts and the programs they run, to the directory that holds the program (or its \fBdotnet\fR subdirectory, when the package has the .NET scripting assembly).
.SH EXIT STATUS
.TP
.B 0
The program ended normally.
.TP
.B 1
The program could not start, or a delegated console operation failed.
.SH EXAMPLES
.PP
Open a saved site:
.RS 4
.EX
linscp "Production web"
.EE
.RE
.PP
Connect to a server given by URL:
.RS 4
.EX
linscp sftp://alice@example.com/var/www/
.EE
.RE
.PP
Upload two files to a site:
.RS 4
.EX
linscp mysite /upload report.pdf notes.txt
.EE
.RE
.PP
Keep a remote directory up to date, starting at once:
.RS 4
.EX
linscp mysite /keepuptodate /home/alice/site /var/www/html /defaults
.EE
.RE
.PP
Use a separate configuration file:
.RS 4
.EX
linscp /ini=/home/alice/work/linscp.ini
.EE
.RE
